# The Indian Monsoon: Onset, Retreat, and Year-to-Year Variation

> India's year turns on a seasonal reversal of winds that delivers
> about three-quarters of its rain in four months. This lesson explains
> the monsoon's machinery, calendar, and notorious variability.

## 1. What "Monsoon" Means

[definition]
The **monsoon** — from the Arabic word *mausim*, season — is a seasonal
reversal of wind direction and rainfall, driven by the temperature
contrast between land and sea. In summer air flows sea-to-land; in
winter, land-to-sea.
[/definition]

## 2. The Machinery

- **Differential heating** — the land warms faster than the ocean,
  drawing a thermal low over north India and Tibet.
- **ITCZ migration** — the equatorial low (doldrums) follows the Sun
  and settles over the Ganga plain by July.
- **Tibetan Plateau heating** — the high plateau acts as a heat pump
  lofting warm air and strengthening the low.
- **Somali (Findlater) jet** — a cross-equatorial stream that feeds
  the Arabian Sea branch with moisture.
- **El Niño** — a warming of the eastern Pacific that typically
  weakens the Indian monsoon.
- **Indian Ocean Dipole** — a positive dipole (western Indian Ocean
  warmer than normal) generally strengthens it.

## 3. The Southwest Monsoon

@diagram tree id="monsoon-branches" layout="tree" aria="Two branches of the southwest monsoon"
  "Southwest monsoon" -> "Arabian Sea branch"
  "Southwest monsoon" -> "Bay of Bengal branch"
  "Arabian Sea branch" -> "Kerala and Western Ghats"
  "Bay of Bengal branch" -> "Meghalaya and the Ganga plain"
@/diagram

The **Arabian Sea branch** strikes Kerala about 1 June, heaving
orographic rain on the windward Western Ghats. The **Bay of Bengal
branch**, deflected westward by the Himalaya, soaks Meghalaya — home
of **Mawsynram**, the wettest place on Earth (~11,870 mm a year) —
before crossing the Ganga plain. The two branches cover the whole
country by about mid-July and retreat by mid-October.

[info title="Why the windward Ghats flood"]
Moisture-laden winds forced up the Ghats cool and condense; the
leeward Deccan sits in a rain shadow that keeps Pune drier than Mumbai.
[/info]

## 4. The Retreating (Northeast) Monsoon

In October–November the land cools, the lows fade, and winds reverse
to blow from the north-east. Crossing the Bay of Bengal, they pick up
moisture and drench the **Tamil Nadu coast**, which depends on this
season for most of its rain. Meanwhile the northern plains swelter
under the humid **October heat**, and Bay of Bengal cyclones strike
the Andhra–Odisha–Tamil Nadu coast.

## 5. The Indian Year in Four Seasons

| Season | Months | Signature |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Cold weather | December–February | North-east trades; western disturbances water the rabi crop |
| Hot weather | March–May | The Loo, dust storms, soaring maximums |
| Southwest monsoon | June–September | About 75% of India's annual rainfall |
| Retreating monsoon | October–November | North-east winds, Tamil Nadu rain, cyclones |

## 6. Agriculture and the Monsoon Gamble

| Crop season | Sown | Harvested | Typical crops |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Kharif | June, with the monsoon | September–October | Rice, jute, cotton, maize |
| Rabi | October–November | March–April | Wheat, barley, mustard, peas |

Kharif yields live or die by the monsoon's timing and vigour; rabi
crops lean on stored moisture and irrigation. Years of excess bring
floods — years of deficit, drought; El Niño years are frequently, but
not automatically, deficient ones.

[warning]
A delayed onset or a long mid-season break can halve kharif output,
which is why the India Meteorological Department's weekly forecasts are
watched like election results.
[/warning]

